  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "The node did not pass: the final gate verdict is **BARE_LITERAL**, caused by a gap in the node itself. Everything else passes. While I temporarily wrote the \"ordinal = 1\" comparison without a literal, the gate returned **OK** on all 6 witnesses.\n\n**The node defect (span s5):** the node reads \"use the left lane\" as \"every lane the subject occupies has ordinal 1\", but it declares no literals. So the formula's `1` (count) is rejected. There is no honest way to say it without the literal:\n- \"Has no left neighbour\" stays unknown, never false, for a leftmost lane, because the neighbour field is simply empty.\n- Building the 1 out of arithmetic, such as `n·n = n ∧ n > 0`, only hides the figure from the check.\n\nI used that encoding for one diagnostic run, then put the plain `ordinal = 1` back so the rule doesn't sneak past the check. The fix belongs to the plan agent: declare a literal on s5 with value 1, unit count, sourced from st.lane_adjacency's contract (\"ordinal 1 is the leftmost lane\"). With that in place, the files should pass as they are.\n\n**The rule as written:**\n- **Subject:** a vehicle, using the aggregate `DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle`.\n- **Applicability:** for some roundabout r, the subject's left turn or U-turn through r is recognized at its exit, judged once at that moment.\n- **Obligation:** the same roundabout and act, and from the subject's latest entry into r up to the exit, every lane it occupies inside r has ordinal 1.\n- **Narrowings:** none of my own; every choice comes from the node.\n\n**Witnesses:** forbid, permit, idle, blind, branch_1 (U-turn from the outer lane, violated) and branch_2 (U-turn from the inner lane, compliant). The gate rejects traces whose heading doesn't follow the yaw rate, so I rebuilt them to be physically consistent:\n- Headings follow from the yaw rate.\n- Positions advance 1 m per 50 ms along the heading.\n- Occupancy is stated from the car's footprint.\n\n**Gate runs (all 6 used):**\n1. BARE_LITERAL\n2. WITNESS_FAIL: the headings didn't follow the yaw rate.\n3. WITNESS_FAIL: the footprint was undefined without a heading.\n4. OK, with the diagnostic encoding.\n5. BUILD_FAIL: a leftover comment from my edit.\n6. BARE_LITERAL, the state I left.\n\nOnce I mistakenly sent a second shell command (the gate command with extra flags). It was denied, did not run, and did not count toward the budget. `task_result.md` in the working directory has the full write-up.",
